:: Thomas Movie Theatre Ethan [nobr]

/* Written by: Winters */
/* Intro */
Deciding you’re in the mood for an animated movie, you approach the only open ticket booth.  Behind the glass, a teenager with a blank expression leans on one elbow. His eyes are glued to his phone screen, but one eye lazily flicks up as you approach.  As you say the name of the film you want to see, he goes back looking down at his phone before mumbling the price.  Pulling out the money, you push it through the open slot in the window.  The teenager doesn’t seem to acknowledge this, and his fingers continue to scroll on his phone.  <br>
<br>
Clearing your throat, you hope to get his attention again, but he continues to scroll on his phone.  Clearing your throat even louder, the teenager gives an exaggerated sigh and puts down his phone to take the money.  He prints off a single ticket and pushes it through slot in the window.  <br>
<br>
<span style="color:LightSteelBlue">“One kiddie movie for the little kid,”</span> he says.  <span style="color:pink">Your face burns a little from embarrassment, but the teenager has already picked his phone back up.</span> Walking away, you hand your ticket to the security guard before heading inside to the showtime.  <br>
<br>
Pushing open the heavy door to the theatre, you leave the lobby's bright lights and enter the dim gloom of the theatre.  A thin, dusty beam of light from the projector cuts through the darkness, highlighting floating specks of dust in the air.  On screen, static images of candy and other advertisements flash.  <br>
<br>
The projector light is bright enough to illuminate the crowd. You’re surprised to see so few families or kids in the crowd.  The crowd is made mostly of adults in their 20s and 30s. They seem excited, and the murmur of several conversations echoes in the cavernous space.  A lot of them are wearing shirts with animated characters or large cute furry creatures.  One large furry creature with grey fur, a rotund belly, pointy ears, and long whiskers seems particularly popular.  Checking your ticket, you confirm that you’re in the right theatre.    <br>
<br>
Looking around, you see two red-headed boys sitting off to one side.  One is about your age, and the other around <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Noah</span>’s.  <span style="color:lightgreen">Relieve that at least one kid is here and around your age, you start to walk over to them.</span>  It isn’t until you get closer that you recognize <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span> in the low light.  Next to him is his little brother <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ethan</span>.    <br>
<br>
As you approach, <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span> turns his head towards you. He jumps a little, and his face flushes red.  He doesn’t seem angry, but embarrassed.  <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ethan</span> just stares at you, a blank expression on his face. <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span> looks around, as if he’s contemplating hiding or running.  Instead, he freezes as you approach his seat. He seems to be acting as if he’s trapped. <span style="color:yellow">You try to alleviate the tension with a casual greeting.</span>  <br>

