/* PC: Everyday, Happy*/
/* Thomas: Everyday, Sad, Blush 1 */
/* Ethan: Everyday */
<span style="color:LightSteelBlue">“Hey,”</span> he mutters. <span style="color:LightSteelBlue">“I didn’t think anyone from school would be here.”</span>  You shrug and tell him that the movie looked interesting.  He seems to relax a little when you say that.  Looking over at the seat next to him he says, <span style="color:LightSteelBlue">“Just sit down if you’re going to. You don’t need my permission. I guess better you than some of the other-”</span> <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span> stops himself. You’re pretty sure he was going to swear, though <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ethan</span>’s presence makes him hold his tongue.  Agreeing, you settle down in the seat next to him.  The seat groans in protest, and you feel the springs dig into you slightly through the worn seat.  <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ethan</span> continues to watch you this whole time.  You give him a little wave, and he stares at you for another moment before moving his eyes towards the screen.  <br>
<br>
Looking at <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span>, he is also watching you.  Deciding to ease the tension, you ask <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span> about his interest in the movie.  This seems to work as he brightens slightly.  <span style="color:LightSteelBlue">“The studio is pretty good, I guess. We have some of them on VHS. <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ethan</span> likes them and they were giving away tickets at the… Well, we got some tickets so figured why not. It’s just a kid’s movie, but I really like the…”</span> <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span> stops himself short suddenly.  He frowns and looks at the screen flashing another advertisement.  This time for Daddi Pepper.  There’s another awkward silence.   <br>
<br>
You bring the subject back to the movie and studio, which seems to work as the silence is broken as <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span> starts talking again. As the conversation goes on, <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span> starts to speak more passionately about the art style, the director, and studio. When you mention that <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span> must have been wanting to see this movie for quite a while, his face flushes with embarrassment.   <br>
<br>
<span style="color:LightSteelBlue">“No, I’m not here to see some kiddie movie. It’s for him,”</span> and he gestures towards <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ethan</span>.  <span style="color:LightSteelBlue">“Thought it’d be good for him not to be in that shithole for a few hours. I can just appreciate when something looks good. Artistically, that is…”</span> he mutters under his breath. You have a suspicion that <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span> is the reason they’re there, and not because it’s a kid’s film for <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ethan</span> to see, but you don’t bring it up.  You do mention the number of adults in the crowd.  <br>
<br>
<span style="color:LightSteelBlue">“They’re just a bunch of overgrown nerds,”</span> he says dismissively.  As he says this he reaches into his sweatshirt and pulls out a small plastic bag. It appears that <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span> has smuggled in some snacks.  Inside are some pretzels that look rather crushed. <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span> opens the bag and hands it over to <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ethan</span>, who starts to idly reach his hand in to pluck out a short pretzel stub. He continues to stare at the screen as he munches on the pretzels.   <br>
<br>
<<linkreplace"Continue">>
    The conversation stops short as the screen starts to show movie previews.  The chatter in the crowd starts to settle down.  As the movie starts with the logo of the film studio, a cheer erupts from the crowd. You’re starting to wonder to yourself what you’ve gotten yourself into.  <span style="color:pink">As the cheers end, you notice that <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ethan</span> curls up closer to <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span>.</span> A swell of some lovely, upbeat music starts with the opening credits. <span style="color:lightgreen">From there the movie pulls you in. The movie is beautiful.  It seems to be a foreign film as everything is subtitled, but the art is meticulously hand drawn. It starts with two brothers moving out to the countryside with their father.  Despite it focusing on the two kids, and being whimsical and magical at times, you’re surprised how natural and mature the film is.</span><br>
    <br>
    <span style="color:pink">There is even a scene with the two brothers bathing nude with their father.  The scene feels natural and sweet as they laugh and play in the bath, none of them showing any modesty. They seem so happy.</span>  Off to the side, you hear a small sigh.  <br>
    <br>
    Looking over at <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span>, his eyes are transfixed to the screen and doesn’t seem to notice you watching.  He has an odd expression on his face.  Part sadness but also yearning. He bites down on his lip, and you wonder if he’s holding back some emotion.  <span style="color:pink">You notice he puts his arm around <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ethan</span>’s shoulder and gives it an affectionate gentle squeeze.  <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ethan</span> leans his head against <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span>’ hand and idly picks at a tear in his seat’s arm cushion.</span>   <br>
    <br>
    <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Thomas</span>’s eyes flicker over to you, and his expression turns back to stoic.  His hand on <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ethan</span>’s shoulder moves down and plucks out a pretzel out of the bag before bringing it to his mouth. You blush slightly at getting caught staring, and you shift your attention back to the film.  <br>

